    done: "the card resolves to a cited catalog row.", needs: []),
  gc_row(id: GcN12e, what: "Install the R9700 pair and run the same leg on its host, with its receipts and selection.",
    home: GcCreates { module: "gunbc.host.gpu_card_converge" },
    done: "both cards' readbacks hold, and the leg gained rows only.", needs: [GcD3, GcN7, GcN8, GcN10, GcN12a, GcN12b, GcN12c, GcN12d]),
  gc_row(id: GcGoal, what: "Every in-bound card converges.", home: GcExtends { symbol: "gunbc.plans.gpu_card_host_program::gpu_card_in_bound" },
    done: "no census row is committed, and every in-bound row's readback holds on its host.", needs: [GcN11, GcN12e]),
]

fn gpu_card_host_program_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    p(text: "Status: **draft, under design with the operator; in review as PR #15207.** Each row carries its standing, and section 7 derives what is ready from them; the converge leg has not started. Section 8 lists the decisions it needs first. The model lands before the converge leg, per DESIGN's model-before-implement rule, and the order is in section 7. The lane sits under the Mt. Collins worker."),
    h2(text: "0. The bound: every in-bound card converges"),
    p(text: "**The bound is one predicate, `gunbc.plans.gpu_card_host_program::gpu_card_in_bound`.** A card is in bound when it is **installed in an operator-owned fleet host, or committed to one**. Whether it is serving at the moment does not matter. Every other card names why it is outside. The program is finished when no census row is still only committed, and every in-bound card's independent readback holds on its host, through the real path. A committed card therefore keeps the program open: it cannot retire around a card that has not arrived yet."),
    p(text: "The working census below is the projection of `gunbc.plans.gpu_card_host_program::gpu_card_working_census`. It is a reading for the operator to confirm (D0). It is not the census authority: N0a creates the census in `gunbc.fleet`, moves the predicate there, and this carrier then imports it."),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "count"), cell(text: "product"), cell(text: "slots"), cell(text: "host"), cell(text: "membership")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: map(gpu_card_working_census, r => gc_census_projection(r: r)),
    },
    p(text: "The bound also limits what gets modelled. Fan zones are needed only where a card is **passive**, which in this census means the A16s in the H11. An actively cooled card needs an accelerator guard reading, not a zone posture. A model, row or measurement that no in-bound card's convergence consumes is a follow-up, not part of this program (DESIGN §3c)."),
    h2(text: "1. The request"),
    p(text: "When a card goes into a host, the fleet should get every one of these right from the model, not from a script someone wrote by hand on that host:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**the driver**: the right package and pinned version for the card's vendor and generation. For the census in section 0 that means NVIDIA for the A16, RTX 2000 Ada and RTX PRO 6000 Workstation, and AMD (ROCm) for the committed R9700 pair, through the same leg;"),
      li(text: "**the card's power states**: an applied power cap inside the range the device reports, persistence, and an idle policy that parks clocks without throwing away warm state;"),
      li(text: "**physical placement**: which slot each card sits in, and which fan is physically plugged into which header;"),
      li(text: "**the fan zones**: which board zone each header belongs to, and which temperatures that zone follows, so that a policy asking for more cooling reaches the fans that actually cool the card;"),
      li(text: "**one converge leg** that observes all of this, applies it, reads it back independently, and refuses with a typed cause when any part does not hold."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "2. What main already carries"),
    p(text: "Most of the vocabulary this program needs already has a home. The program extends those homes and adds new ones only where no home exists (DESIGN §3b)."),
    TableBlock {
      header: t4(a: "concern", b: "home on main", c: "what it holds", d: "gap this program fills"),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        t4(a: "board slots", b: "`extdeps.boards.supermicro_h11ssl::supermicro_h11ssl_pcie_slots`", c: "SLOT1–6 and their lane counts (2, 4 and 6 are x16; 1, 3 and 5 are x8)", d: "physical order and spacing (whether a dual-slot passive card in one slot blocks or starves its neighbour), and which CPU root complex each slot hangs off"),
        t4(a: "board fan headers", b: "`extdeps.boards.supermicro_h11ssl::supermicro_h11ssl_fan_headers`", c: "FAN1–5 and FANA–B, plus their pinout", d: "**which zone each header belongs to.** The numbered headers being zone 0 and the lettered ones zone 1 is read off the hand-written controller, not cited to the manual yet"),
        t4(a: "what is plugged into a header", b: "`gunbc.fleet.fleet_fan_wiring`, `gunbc.fleet.fleet_fan_tach_expectation`", c: "header to installed fan, its model standing (`InstalledFanModelStanding`), and where tach is expected, for srv1–srv4", d: "no H11 host row. A daisy chain (3 × 120 mm on FANB) is several fans behind one tach. The module states that \"which PWM endpoint CONTROLS a header\" is not modelled; that is the zone layer"),
        t4(a: "fleet fan intent", b: "`product.fleet_fan_policy::fleet_fan_policy`", c: "`FirmwareAutomatic` is the only resting posture; a held duty floor is a guarded excursion; the guard roles are SoC, DIMM, VRD and outlet", d: "**there is no accelerator guard role, and no resting posture in which a host-side loop follows GPU temperature.** The H11 controller sits outside the fleet ruling today (section 5.4, decision D1)"),
        t4(a: "fan converge routes", b: "`gunbc.fleet.fleet_fan_converge_routes`", c: "an OpenBMC route that is bound, and a MegaRAC route that is unbound", d: "`extdeps.bmc.endpoint::BmcFirmwareFamily` has no Supermicro arm, and there is no model of Supermicro's OEM fan-mode and zone-duty commands"),
        t4(a: "ipmitool transport", b: "`extdeps.bmc.ipmi`", c: "one operation per command, including a `raw` precedent (`MasterWriteReadAuthenticated`)", d: "the Supermicro OEM operations belong in their own module, which binds to this transport"),
        t4(a: "GPU catalog", b: "`extdeps.gpu.types::GpuModelCatalogRow`, `extdeps.gpu.nvidia`", c: "one TDP per row; rows for the RTX PRO 6000 (Workstation, Max-Q, Server), PRO 5000, 5090, B200, B300 and H200 NVL; `GpuRuntime` already includes `Rocm`", d: "no rows for the A2, T10, RTX 2000 Ada or R9700, and no AMD GPU module. There is no field for a configurable power range (A2 40–60 W, PRO 6000 Server 400–600 W). The A16 (`extdeps.gpu.nvidia_a16`) is deliberately not a catalog row, because the board carries four GPUs, so a board-of-N-GPUs shape is missing"),
        t4(a: "nvidia-smi", b: "`extdeps.nvidia.system_management_interface`", c: "`QueryComputeApps` only, with a parse that cannot turn a refusal into zero", d: "no power, clock, P-state or persistence queries, and no `-pl`, `-pm`, `-lmc`/`-rmc`, `-lgc`/`-rgc` or supported-clock operations"),
        t4(a: "NVML / CUDA", b: "`extdeps.nvidia.management_library`, `extdeps.nvidia.cuda_driver`", c: "device-memory observation that can refuse; a modelled attribute query", d: "nothing for driver packaging, versions, open versus proprietary kernel module, or `nvidia-persistenced`"),
        t4(a: "watt semantics", b: "`gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy`", c: "the catalog TDP and an applied limit are distinct figures; an applied limit above TDP is refused", d: "the device-reported **minimum** (48.75 W per A16 GPU) is a third figure. It is a reading of the card, not the catalog's TDP and not a policy choice"),
        t4(a: "operating-point selection", b: "`product.mt_collins_power_policy` (first consumer of `std.decision`)", c: "selection of a fleet operating point, with a receipt", d: "the per-card cap and park choice should be another consumer of the same selection, not a constant written into a policy row"),
        t4(a: "host provisioning pattern", b: "`gunbc.runner.runner_browser_toolchain` (#12500); #15177 (open, `gunbc.runner.runner_gpu_container_toolchain`)", c: "an observe / converge / readback leg that runs as a fleet-converge mode", d: "#15177 states that **the driver is never pinned anywhere in the install path**: the toolkit leg assumes some driver is already present. This program is where that driver comes from"),
        t4(a: "host identity", b: "`gunbc.fleet.fleet_host_identity`", c: "srv1–4, srv9–16, and opaque host-xxxxxx identities", d: "the H11 A16 host (192.168.1.202) has no identity row. There are `gunbc.machine_intake.supermicro_h11ssl_*` receipts for H11SSL units, keyed by BMC MAC"),
      ]
    },
    h2(text: "3. What was measured, and where it must land"),
    p(text: "These are receipts: they record what this repository observed on one host, so they belong in the observing layer (DESIGN §3, external upstream decomposition). A device's reported minimum power limit is an observation of the device. It becomes an upstream fact only once it is cited to NVIDIA."),
    TableBlock {
      header: t3(a: "reading (A16, one GPU unless stated)", b: "figure", c: "standing"),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        t3(a: "no process", b: "P8, 13.6–15.6 W, 210 / 405 MHz", c: "measured, range across GPUs"),
        t3(a: "bare CUDA context, no work", b: "P0, 27.0 W, 1755 / 6251 MHz", c: "measured. This happens with any context, not only Strata's"),
        t3(a: "Strata loaded, idle", b: "P0, 27.0 W", c: "measured"),
        t3(a: "Strata loaded, memory clock locked to its minimum", b: "P8, about 12.5–15.6 W, warm state retained, wake about 1–2 s", c: "measured. The wake time is approximate"),
        t3(a: "minimum power limit", b: "48.75 W, maximum 62.50 W. `-pl 40` exits 2 with \"not a valid power limit which should be between 48.75 W and 62.50 W\" and leaves the cap unchanged", c: "measured on driver 595.91.07; not cited to NVIDIA"),
        t3(a: "supported clocks, GPU 0", b: "memory 405 or 6251 MHz; at 405 MHz memory, graphics 405–420 MHz. `-lmc 405,405` accepted at runtime; `-lmi` is not a recognized option (exit 255)", c: "measured on driver 595.91.07"),
        t3(a: "48.75 W against the stock 62.5 W, in this chassis", b: "same throughput: the cards hit their thermal limit before the cap", c: "measured on one cooling configuration"),
        t3(a: "core clock locked to 900 MHz", b: "about 20% lower throughput", c: "measured"),
        t3(a: "warm decode bottleneck, 16 GB engine", b: "about 60% memory bandwidth, about 38% SM; PCIe about 0.5 GB/s average with 7 GB/s bursts", c: "measured, one sensitivity run"),
        t3(a: "lid off", b: "87–92 °C at idle, then Xid 154 on six GPUs", c: "incident"),
        t3(a: "two boards in adjacent slots", b: "96–99 °C under load at minimum clocks; one GPU dropped off the bus", c: "incident"),
        t3(a: "SLOT2 + SLOT6, Ada removed, 3 × 120 mm on FANB, idle and partial load", b: "48–81 °C", c: "measured"),
        t3(a: "same placement, full load, FANB at 100%", b: "the first GPU of each board (bus 85/86 and c5/c6) reaches 87–88 °C with SW thermal slowdown (210–585 MHz); the other GPUs stay at 56–78 °C", c: "measured. Heat is not uniform within a board: it depends on the GPU's position"),
        t3(a: "installed driver on the H11", b: "`nvidia-driver-595-server-open` 595.91.07-0ubuntu0.24.04.1 (server branch, open kernel modules), on Ubuntu 24.04", c: "read from dpkg"),
        t3(a: "BMC and fan state on the H11", b: "Supermicro BMC firmware 1.52 on an H11SSL-i/c/nc; fan mode reads 0x01 (Full); zone 0 duty 0x1d (29%), zone 1 duty 0x64 (100%); the specimen loop writes zone duty with `raw 0x30 0x70 0x66 0x01 <zone> <duty>`", c: "read in-band through ipmitool"),
        t3(a: "RTX 2000 Ada, idle with the engine loaded", b: "4.1–4.2 W per card on one reading; about 4–8 W across readings. It downclocks without help", c: "measured"),
      ]
    },
    p(text: "The two incidents are what force slot spacing and fan zones into the model. Adjacency is a placement fact that predicted a failure, and the lid-off Xid shows the harm of running a passive card under a zone that does not follow its temperature. Each incident files or updates a `gunbc.recurring_failure_mode` row (DESIGN §4b). Receipts for the other in-bound cards (the RTX PRO 6000 Workstation, the RTX 2000 Ada pair, and the R9700s once they are installed) do not exist yet. Section 6 is the instrument that produces them, keyed by installed position."),
    h2(text: "4. The hand-written state this program replaces"),
    p(text: "These are specimens. They are not a template to copy. The converge leg deletes each of them when it takes over its job (delete-first, DESIGN §3), and each one's behaviour becomes a control the leg must reproduce."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "`/usr/local/sbin/a16-fan.sh` + `a16-fan.service` puts the BMC in full mode (Supermicro raw `0x30 0x45`) and writes zone duty (`0x30 0x70 0x66`). Zone 0 follows the CPU and zone 1 follows the hottest GPU. A hand-edited override file, `/etc/a16-fan.zone0`, can pin zone 0."),
      li(text: "`/usr/local/sbin/a16-idle-memclock.sh` + `a16-idle-memclock.service` is the idle memory-clock parker."),
      li(text: "The `h11-strata@<bus>.service` template applies the 48.75 W cap at start, matching the A16 by PCI ID `0x25b6`. The cap belongs to the card's power policy, not to the engine unit."),
      li(text: "Persistence is set by hand with `nvidia-smi -pm 1`, and the setting is lost on reboot. `nvidia-persistenced` is running, but the unit Ubuntu ships in `nvidia-compute-utils-595-server` is `static` and starts the daemon with `--no-persistence-mode`. So the daemon is up with persistence explicitly off, and every boot reads `Disabled` until someone runs `-pm 1`."),
    ]),
    p(text: "The engine units themselves, `strata-pool.service` on srv3 and the router configuration, are serving deployment, not card provisioning. Section 9 covers them."),
    h2(text: "5. The model, by layer"),
    h3(text: "5.1 Upstream facts (extdeps): one module per independently versioned upstream"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**`extdeps.nvidia.system_management_interface`** (extended, since it is the same upstream): power queries (`power.draw`, `power.limit`, `power.min_limit`, `power.max_limit`, `pstate`, `clocks.gr`, `clocks.mem`, `persistence_mode`), `--query-supported-clocks`, `clocks_event_reasons.active`, and the effects `-pl`, `-pm`, `-lmc`/`-rmc` and `-lgc`/`-rgc`. Each operation's \"not supported\" output is its own arm, as `QueryComputeApps` already does, and so is an option the installed driver does not recognize. The option surface is a fact of the driver release (N2a), not of nvidia-smi in general: `-lmi` appears in NVIDIA's documentation, but 595.91.07 rejects it with exit 255."),
      li(text: "**`extdeps.nvidia.persistence_daemon`** (new): the startup persistence mode an `nvidia-persistenced` argv selects, as NVIDIA documents the flags. What a distribution's packaged unit passes is not NVIDIA's: on Ubuntu's 595 server packages the unit is `static` and runs `--no-persistence-mode`, which is a receipt of the observing layer. Reading a unit's `ExecStart` argv is systemd's, and it is two facts: `extdeps.systemd.systemctl::systemctl_show_property_value` retrieves the property, and `extdeps.systemd.service_exec::exec_command_line` owns the command-line grammar its tokenization inverts. So whether the daemon is running says nothing about whether persistence is on."),
      li(text: "**`extdeps.nvidia.linux_driver`** (new): driver branches and releases, the distribution package names, open versus proprietary kernel module, and the supported-GPU list per release, each cited to NVIDIA's release notes. It holds no version pin: a pin is policy."),
      li(text: "**`extdeps.amd.amdgpu_driver`** and **`extdeps.amd.rocm`** (new), plus **`extdeps.amd.amd_smi`** (new): the kernel driver and firmware the R9700 needs, the ROCm release that supports the card and on which distributions, and the power-cap and clock operations. The R9700's figures (architecture, memory, board power, PCIe) are read obligations until they are cited. None is transcribed here from memory."),
      li(text: "**Catalog rows**: every in-bound model not yet catalogued, which is the RTX 2000 Ada in `extdeps.gpu.nvidia` and the R9700 in a new `extdeps.gpu.amd`. `extdeps.gpu.types` gains a **configurable power range** distinct from TDP, the board's **zero-power capability** (the A16 brief states it is not supported, so a park policy cannot select it), and a **multi-GPU board** shape so the A16 can be catalogued as one board of four GA107-890 GPUs without pretending it is one GPU. A board row exists only through an admission that refuses a board with no GPU, an inverted range, or a default or named point outside it. The catalog owns capability ranges. It never owns an operating point."),
      li(text: "**`extdeps.bmc.supermicro_oem_fan`** (new): Supermicro's OEM IPMI fan-mode get/set (`0x30 0x45`, with modes Standard, Full, Optimal, HeavyIO and PUE, read before they are typed) and per-zone duty get/set (`0x30 0x70 0x66`), bound to the `extdeps.bmc.ipmi` transport. Their byte format is not theirs: `extdeps.bmc.ipmitool_raw_reply` (new) owns the raw reply's bytes and a request byte's spelling, and the existing master-write-read and SMpro readers move onto it. Separately, `extdeps.bmc.endpoint::BmcFirmwareFamily` gains a Supermicro arm, so the operations are selected by the host's BMC."),
      li(text: "**`extdeps.boards.supermicro_h11ssl`** (extended): the two cooling zones, the fan headers and the BIOS slot labels the manual states. The manual does not state which headers each zone drives, or the physical slot order and pitch, so those are typed read obligations, each discharged by its own row."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "5.2 Physical placement (gunbc.fleet): what is plugged into what, on which host"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "An identity row for the H11 host. Under the 2026-10-07 rule it is opaque, not \"h11\"."),
      li(text: "**Card residence**: which card (by serial, with its catalog row) sits in which slot of which host, and for a multi-GPU board which GPU sits at which position. The shape extends `product.placement_supply::PhysicalContainment`, whose `HostContainsComponent` already binds a component to a host but not to a slot; the rows sit beside the existing containments in `gunbc.fleet.fleet_physical_inventory`. The census reads an installed card's host from here rather than holding a second copy. Adjacency is derived from the board's slot order, never authored, so that \"two passive boards in adjacent slots\" is a refusable placement and not something that is only discovered later."),
      li(text: "**Fan residence** extends `gunbc.fleet.fleet_fan_wiring`: each header's fans, how many, and their topology (direct, splitter or daisy chain). The tach expectation follows from the topology, so a chain is rotation expected with tach unreadable, which is a different state from a dead fan."),
      li(text: "**Zone reach** is derived: header to zone from the board, joined with fan residence. The answer is \"zone 1 on this host drives these fans, and they cool these slots\". Reach resolves to **a GPU position inside a board, not only to a slot**: at full load the first GPU of each A16 board throttles while its siblings on the same board stay 10–30 °C cooler, so a zone that follows only a per-slot or per-host maximum responds to the wrong population. The last part, which fans cool which positions, is an operator declaration with its own standing, because nothing reads airflow."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "5.3 Card power states (gunbc.serving): a state machine, not a set of constants"),
    TableBlock {
      header: t4(a: "state", b: "GPU and model", c: "wake cost", d: "what survives"),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: [
        t4(a: "Hot", b: "context open, normal clocks", c: "none", d: "weights, expert cache, KV"),
        t4(a: "ClockParked", b: "context open, minimum supported clocks", c: "about 1–3 s (A16: about 1–2 s, measured)", d: "everything in VRAM"),
        t4(a: "EngineStopped", b: "no CUDA context; driver persisted", c: "seconds to minutes", d: "host and disk caches only"),
        t4(a: "HostOff", b: "whole serving cell down", c: "tens of seconds to minutes", d: "durable host state only"),
      ]
    },
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**The operating point is selected per installed realization, not per model.** The full-load receipt shows the first GPU on each A16 board throttling while its siblings on the same board run 10–30 °C cooler. So the key is the installed realization: host, slot, GPU position on the board, fan reach, driver, engine and workload. The catalog supplies the admissible range, and the device-reported [min, max] narrows it. The applied cap, the park threshold and its hysteresis are then a `std.decision` selection over measured curves: throughput, card energy, whole-host wall energy and wake cost. `gunbc.serving.home_serving_power_policy` already refuses an applied limit above TDP. The result is a `SelectionReceipt`, the same shape `product.mt_collins_power_policy` produces, and a change to any keyed fact makes it a different decision."),
      li(text: "**The park clock is a reading, never a constant.** The minimum comes from `--query-supported-clocks` for that card. 405 MHz is the A16's reading on 595.91.07, not a fleet number. Whether a lock takes effect at runtime is established by the effect itself, not by a capability query: the park is admitted for a card once a `-lmc` lock to its minimum is followed by a `clocks.mem` readback equal to the lock, and a reset is followed by a readback above it. When the lock refuses, or the readback disagrees, parking for that card refuses; it does not fall back to a power cap."),
      li(text: "**A power cap is not an idle control.** A cap constrains the card under load. Idle power is governed by context, clocks, firmware and fans, so the model keeps them as two separate fields, and a policy cannot claim idle savings from a cap."),
      li(text: "**Break-even is a fold, not prose:** `t = E_wake / (P_old − P_new)`. On the A16, ClockParked and no-process both read about 15.5 W, so the denominator for stopping the engine is about zero, and stopping the engine never wins on GPU energy alone. The fold must refuse when the denominator is not positive. It must not report infinity as a recommendation."),
      li(text: "**Wake is part of the contract:** reset the clocks, read back that they reset, then dispatch. A wake that returns without that readback has not converged (DESIGN §3d)."),
      li(text: "**Fleet consolidation**, meaning which replica to wake and when to shut a whole host down, belongs to the router and the serving fleet program, not to this program. This program supplies the per-card states and their measured costs, which that program consumes."),
    ]),
    h3(text: "5.4 Fan zones driven by policy: the conflict with the fleet ruling"),
    p(text: "`product.fleet_fan_policy` rests every host in `FirmwareAutomatic` (ruling `gunbc.design_ruling fan_policy_is_one_fleet_management_workflow`), and every guard trips **toward automatic**. On the H11 that is the wrong direction for the GPU zone. The Supermicro firmware does not follow the temperature of a passive A16, so automatic control does not track the part that is heating, and the lid-off Xid is what that costs. The hand-written controller already breaks the ruling: it runs the BMC in full mode and has a host loop write zone duty."),
    p(text: "**A fail-safe has to hold when the thing that failed was the host.** A host-side loop can write full duty after a lost reading or an ordinary exit. It cannot write anything after the loop, or the whole host, has died, and a witness over a modelled death does not establish the physical transition. So D1 has two honest options:"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**(b) A fixed firmware posture.** Every zone that reaches a passive card rests in a Supermicro firmware mode (Full, or HeavyIO if its reading shows it follows enough). The BMC holds that posture with the host dead, because no host process is in the loop. The cost is acoustic."),
      li(text: "**(a) A host loop under a lease enforced outside the host.** The loop may lower duty only while it holds a lease. An enforcer that is not on the host, either a BMC capability or a fleet process reaching the BMC over its own network, writes full duty when the lease expires. The enforcer does not exist, so the graph in section 7 does not carry it: the graph is the graph of option (b), and choosing (a) is a revision of this carrier that adds the enforcer before the posture is realized."),
    ]),
    p(text: "Either way the model gains an **accelerator guard role**, so the posture is read against the GPUs it protects. What the firmware does when no host writes is typed as unread in `extdeps.bmc.supermicro_oem_fan`, because both options rest on that fact, and the posture's readback with the host's controller stopped is what reads it. The recommendation is (b) for this program's bound, because it is realizable with what exists. Option (a) is a follow-up that buys back acoustics."),
    h3(text: "5.5 The converge leg (gunbc.host): observe, apply, read back, refuse"),
    p(text: "It follows the pattern of #12500 and #15177 as one fleet-converge mode, regenerated into the workflow by the model:"),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: "**Observe**: PCI IDs and slot of each device (from `/sys/bus/pci/slots` joined with the board's slot table), loaded driver and version, every nvidia-smi or amd-smi reading in 5.1, persistence unit state, BMC fan mode and zone duty, and any residue from the specimens in section 4."),
      li(text: "**Plan**: compare desired with observed and decide Noop, Apply or Refuse for each fact. The plan never chooses a cap or curve itself (§3d); those arrive as selection receipts."),
      li(text: "**Apply**: install the pinned driver packages, enable persistence (with a drop-in where the unit is `static`), override the daemon's `ExecStart` with a drop-in that drops `--no-persistence-mode`, install a boot-time oneshot that applies the selected cap, put the zone posture in place, and remove the specimens. Nothing written to the host is hand-authored."),
      li(text: "**Read back** from a second, independent observation: the **loaded** driver version equals the pin (the installed package is not enough); the cap equals the selection and sits within [min, max]; persistence reads `Enabled` after a reboot; a park and wake cycle moves the P-state both ways; and the zone posture reads back from the BMC."),
    ]),
    p(text: "**A driver transition is disruptive, so it is a typed result, not an assumed live apply.** Installing a new branch or changing the kernel-module flavour can require stopping engines, unloading modules or rebooting before the loaded-driver readback can hold. The leg then returns `DrainRequired` or `RebootRequired` for that host. It never reboots or unloads under an engine that still holds seats. Placement withdraws the host's seats (N14), the leg performs the transition, and a **later, independent pass** reads the loaded driver back. Placement readmits the host only after that pass holds. A pass that observes a pending transition reports it. It does not report convergence."),
    p(text: "Typed refusals include: the device is not in the catalog; the driver does not support the device; the loaded driver differs from the pin; a drain or reboot is required but the host still holds seats; the cap is outside the catalog or device range; the park clock is not runtime-modifiable; a header is unmapped to any zone; a slot is unmapped to the board; adjacent passive cards; a zone that reaches a passive card is not in the chosen posture; a specimen is still present after apply. Each one is located at the card, slot, GPU position or header."),
    h2(text: "6. The measurement instrument: one matrix, every card"),
    p(text: "A `gunbc test <label>` row, created by N7, not a one-off script. It is keyed by installed position, not by model, and every sample carries whole-host wall power from N13. Three parts:"),
    CodeBlock { code: "A  idle phases (each 60 s unless stated)
   A1 no client (persistence on)
   A2 bare CUDA/HIP context
   A3 context + 1 GiB allocation
   A4 engine loaded, idle (2-5 min)
   A5 clock-parked (min read from --query-supported-clocks; admitted by lock + readback)
   A6 wake: reset clocks, one warm request -> time to normal clocks + first useful token
   A7 engine stopped, driver persisted (the floor)
B  active power-cap sweep under a representative warm load
   caps from the device min to max in fixed steps; at each: throughput, card energy,
   wall energy, temperature, clock-event reasons -> one throughput-versus-energy curve
C  idle-gap distribution from the router's request trace
   -> the inputs for the park threshold and its hysteresis (minimum dwell, so the card does not thrash)

per sample: host, slot, board position, card + VBIOS, driver + module flavour, persistence,
            engine state, P-state, SM/mem clock, card power + energy counter, temperature,
            zone duty/rpm, whole-host wall power and its standing, CPU package power" },
    p(text: "Whole-host wall power is mandatory. Saving 10 W on a card is not a deep-sleep strategy if the host idles at 250 W, so a sample without a wall reading is a refused receipt, not a partial one. The instrument runs on every in-bound installed position, including the R9700s once they are installed."),
    h2(text: "7. The dependency graph"),
    p(text: "The graph is the typed rows `gunbc.plans.gpu_card_host_program::gpu_card_graph_rows`, and this table is their projection. Each change node is one PR. A decision node is the operator's. G is the bound from section 0. \"Needs\" lists completion prerequisites, and the test.claim witness checks that every edge names a row, that the graph is acyclic, and that every node is an ancestor of G. So nothing that G does not need is in the graph."),
    TableBlock {
      header: row(cells: [cell(text: "node"), cell(text: "what"), cell(text: "home"), cell(text: "needs"), cell(text: "standing")]),
      alignments: [AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone, AlignNone],
      rows: map(gpu_card_graph_rows, r => gc_row_projection(r: r)),
    },
    p(text: join(["Nodes that can start now, folded from the rows' standings: ", gc_ready_labels(rows: gpu_card_graph_rows), ". The critical path to the NVIDIA cards runs through card residence, zone reach, the instrument, the policy and the leg: N6b, N6d, N7, N8, N10, N11. On the R9700 side, G also waits on D3 and the cards physically arriving. N10 is designed for the R9700 from the start: if adding it needs anything beyond rows, N10 was built wrong."], "")),
    p(text: "**The graph is complete for the recommended decisions, and says where it is not.** Two answers would change it, and each is a revision of this carrier before the node it reaches starts, not an implicit node: D1 choosing (a) adds the off-host enforcer, and a host whose BMC reports no power-supply input adds a metered-PDU source for that host. Each row has one home; a row whose work spans two modules was split, so N3, N4, N5 and N6 are families."),
    p(text: "**Why the graph rows are not shared with the serving fleet program's rows.** `gunbc.plans.serving_fleet_program::ServingFleetRow` and the row type here have the same skeleton (id, home, edges, standing, done), and this is the second instance, so by DESIGN §6b it should be generalized. Not in this change: lifting the shape into `gunbc.plan` changes a sibling plan and its witness, which is outside this program's bound. The trigger is this PR's merge, and the follow-up is one shared plan-graph row type in `gunbc.plan` with both plans and both witnesses migrated onto it."),
    p(text: "Done means executed (DESIGN §5): G is a readback on each host, not a merged PR. And N10 counts only when discriminating inputs go red on the real path: a cap below the device minimum, a header with no zone, adjacent passive cards, and a reboot requested on a host that still holds seats each refuse."),
    h2(text: "8. Decisions for the operator"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**D1, fan posture for zones that reach a passive card** (section 5.4). (a) A host loop under a lease that is enforced outside the host. (b) A fixed firmware posture (Full or HeavyIO) that holds with the host dead. *Recommend (b)* for this bound: it is realizable today, and (a) needs an enforcer that does not exist yet. Either option amends the fleet fan ruling, because the resting posture is no longer `FirmwareAutomatic`."),
      li(text: "**D2, driver pin.** Which NVIDIA branch (production or new-feature), and open or proprietary kernel modules, for the in-bound mix of Ampere (A16), Ada and Blackwell. *Recommend* one production branch whose release notes list every in-bound card, with open modules if they support all of them. This is to be checked against the release notes, not assumed. The H11 already runs the 595 server branch with open modules (`nvidia-driver-595-server-open`), which is the natural candidate to check first."),
      li(text: "**D0, the installed hosts.** Confirm the host of each installed card whose host section 0 holds as unconfirmed: the A16 pair and the RTX 2000 Ada pair. The R9700 pair's host is D3's, not D0's. Card residence records the answer, and the census reads it from there."),
      li(text: "**D3, R9700 host.** Which host the two R9700s go into, and therefore which kernel and distribution the AMD rows have to support. The census holds them as committed, so the program cannot retire before they converge."),
      li(text: "**D4, who owns park and wake.** A host watcher, the engine, or placement driving a host actuator. *Recommend* placement-driven state with a host actuator and readback, so that park and wake are visible to the consolidation policy rather than hidden beneath it. N8 needs this decision."),
      li(text: "**Lane shape.** One lane under the Mt. Collins worker, working the graph in section 7. Nothing outside the graph is in scope."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "9. Boundaries: what this program does not own"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "Engine deployment (Strata and vLLM units, ports, memory slices) and the pool router, including reconciling the hand edit to the live router into #15113, belong to the serving fleet program (`gunbc.plans.serving_fleet_program`). This program hands it card states and their costs."),
      li(text: "The container toolkit belongs to #15177. That leg should consume this program's driver readback as a prerequisite rather than assume a driver."),
      li(text: "Fleet-wide consolidation and host power-off are routing policy. This program supplies the per-card ladder and its measured wake costs."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data gpu_card_host_program_plan: Plan = Plan {
  slug: "gpu-card-host-program",
  title: "GPU card hosting: drivers, power states, placement and fan zones",
  body: gpu_card_host_program_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Dissolves at G in section 7. No census row is still only committed, and every in-bound card converges through one leg, with its independent readback, the loaded driver included, holding on its host. The leg's refusals have been exercised on the real path: a cap below the device minimum, a header with no zone, adjacent passive cards, and a reboot requested under held seats. None of the section-4 hand-written specimens remains, and every installed position's operating point is a selection receipt rather than a literal. Then this document's content lives in the model's types and the document is deleted."),
  },
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly { ruling: project_documentation_projections_are_not_checkout_artifacts },
}
